From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: TheDBP <TheDBP@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 04:57:11 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] hwui: never block a binder thread on the RenderThread's draw
 lock

The launcher and SystemUI freeze for exactly 4 s, then everything queued
during the freeze lands at once. A trace with binder driver events and
syscalls shows the cycle:

  1. RenderThread takes mFrameMetricsReporterMutex for the whole of draw()
     ("Fix crash from asynchronous GPU metrics", Android 13). This device's
     Adreno driver has no EGL_EXT_buffer_age (debug.hwui.use_buffer_age is
     off), so the window buffer is not dequeued in getFrame() before the
     lock but lazily inside draw(). RenderThread sits in dequeueBuffer,
     holding the lock, waiting for SurfaceFlinger to release a buffer.
  2. SurfaceFlinger's oneway onTransactionCompleted arrives on a binder
     thread. It releases its buffer, then calls the surface-stats listener,
     CanvasContext::onSurfaceStatsAvailable, which blocks on the same mutex.
  3. That thread has not returned, so it has not sent BC_FREE_BUFFER. The
     binder driver therefore parks every later oneway transaction to this
     process in async_todo -- including the next onTransactionCompleted,
     the one carrying the release RenderThread is waiting for.
  4. Nothing moves until the 4000 ms dequeueBuffer timeout fires;
     RenderThread drops the lock, the binder thread frees, the parked
     callbacks flush, and the frames drawn meanwhile all present together.

trace: futex(0x77d5c703b8) held by RenderThread from before dequeueBuffer
(3548.4966) until after "flush commands" (3552.5297); binder:2173_2 waits
on it from 3548.5095, frees its transaction at 3552.5757, and only then
receives the callbacks SurfaceFlinger sent during the stall.

Do not wait on a binder thread. Hand the stats to the RenderThread, which
owns the frame data anyway (the pre-13 code ran finishFrame there). A
weak token tells the queued task if the context died first; both run on
the RenderThread, so the check cannot race the destructor.

Devices whose drivers dequeue in getFrame() never saw this, which is why
it is invisible on modern hardware and absent from lineage-19.1.
---
 libs/hwui/renderthread/CanvasContext.cpp | 38 +++++++++++++++++-------
 libs/hwui/renderthread/CanvasContext.h   |  5 ++++
 2 files changed, 32 insertions(+), 11 deletions(-)

diff --git a/libs/hwui/renderthread/CanvasContext.cpp b/libs/hwui/renderthread/CanvasContext.cpp
index f56d19bfcea0..c74afb7fc983 100644
--- a/libs/hwui/renderthread/CanvasContext.cpp
+++ b/libs/hwui/renderthread/CanvasContext.cpp
@@ -761,17 +761,33 @@ void CanvasContext::onSurfaceStatsAvailable(void* context, int32_t surfaceContro
     }
     uint64_t frameNumber = functions.getFrameNumberFunc(stats);
 
-    FrameInfo* frameInfo = instance->getFrameInfoFromLast4(frameNumber, surfaceControlId);
-
-    if (frameInfo != nullptr) {
-        std::scoped_lock lock(instance->mFrameMetricsReporterMutex);
-        frameInfo->set(FrameInfoIndex::FrameCompleted) = std::max(gpuCompleteTime,
-                frameInfo->get(FrameInfoIndex::SwapBuffersCompleted));
-        frameInfo->set(FrameInfoIndex::GpuCompleted) = std::max(
-                gpuCompleteTime, frameInfo->get(FrameInfoIndex::CommandSubmissionCompleted));
-        instance->mJankTracker.finishFrame(*frameInfo, instance->mFrameMetricsReporter, frameNumber,
-                                           surfaceControlId);
-    }
+    // This runs on a binder thread, inside SurfaceFlinger's oneway onTransactionCompleted
+    // callback, and mFrameMetricsReporterMutex is held by the RenderThread for the whole of
+    // draw(). Blocking here is not a matter of latency: while this thread sleeps it has not
+    // freed the transaction buffer, so the binder driver parks every later oneway callback to
+    // this process -- including the one carrying the next buffer release. If the RenderThread
+    // is inside draw() waiting in dequeueBuffer for exactly that release, nothing moves until
+    // the 4 s dequeue timeout. Drivers with EGL_EXT_buffer_age dequeue in getFrame(), before
+    // the lock, and never hit this; ones without it (buffer age off, swap behaviour Preserved)
+    // dequeue lazily inside draw() and deadlock. So never wait: hand the work to the
+    // RenderThread, which owns this data anyway. The token tells the queued task whether
+    // the context is still alive when it runs.
+    std::weak_ptr<bool> alive = instance->mSurfaceStatsToken;
+    instance->mRenderThread.queue().post(
+            [instance, alive, frameNumber, surfaceControlId, gpuCompleteTime]() {
+                if (alive.expired()) return;
+                FrameInfo* frameInfo =
+                        instance->getFrameInfoFromLast4(frameNumber, surfaceControlId);
+                if (frameInfo == nullptr) return;
+                std::scoped_lock lock(instance->mFrameMetricsReporterMutex);
+                frameInfo->set(FrameInfoIndex::FrameCompleted) = std::max(
+                        gpuCompleteTime, frameInfo->get(FrameInfoIndex::SwapBuffersCompleted));
+                frameInfo->set(FrameInfoIndex::GpuCompleted) = std::max(
+                        gpuCompleteTime,
+                        frameInfo->get(FrameInfoIndex::CommandSubmissionCompleted));
+                instance->mJankTracker.finishFrame(*frameInfo, instance->mFrameMetricsReporter,
+                                                   frameNumber, surfaceControlId);
+            });
 }
 
 // Called by choreographer to do an RT-driven animation
diff --git a/libs/hwui/renderthread/CanvasContext.h b/libs/hwui/renderthread/CanvasContext.h
index d85e579e371c..e84aa600ecec 100644
--- a/libs/hwui/renderthread/CanvasContext.h
+++ b/libs/hwui/renderthread/CanvasContext.h
@@ -40,6 +40,7 @@
 #include <utils/Mutex.h>
 
 #include <functional>
+#include <memory>
 #include <future>
 #include <set>
 #include <string>
@@ -318,6 +319,10 @@ private:
             GUARDED_BY(mFrameMetricsReporterMutex);
     std::mutex mFrameMetricsReporterMutex;
 
+    // Liveness token for work onSurfaceStatsAvailable hands to the RenderThread. Dies with the
+    // context, on the RenderThread, so a queued task that finds it expired must not touch this.
+    std::shared_ptr<bool> mSurfaceStatsToken = std::make_shared<bool>(true);
+
     std::set<RenderNode*> mPrefetchedLayers;
 
     // Stores the bounds of the main content.
